WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.120
- Welcome everybody.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.640
Thank you for coming on
this nicest day of the year

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.822
so far I think.

00:00:16.822 --> 00:00:19.390
Our Schulman lecturer for today

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.665
is professor Ian Phillips.

00:00:21.665 --> 00:00:25.480
Ian has taught in the Philosophy
Departments at Oxford,

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.320
where I think he got his
undergraduate degree,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.220
at University College London,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.370
where I believe he got his PhD,

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.090
in the University of Birmingham

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.240
and he's also taught in the
cognitive science program

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.353
at Princeton.

00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:38.918
For the last several years,

00:00:38.918 --> 00:00:40.580
he has been the Bloomberg
Distinguished Professor

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.360
at Johns Hopkins University.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.750
And this is not just a fancy title,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.891
but it's actually sort of a
new, exciting kind of thing.

00:00:47.891 --> 00:00:50.704
Bloomberg professors must
be fully cross appointed

00:00:50.704 --> 00:00:52.770
in multiple departments.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.961
And so Ian is a professor of philosophy

00:00:55.961 --> 00:01:00.327
and also a professor of
psychological and brain sciences.

00:01:00.327 --> 00:01:04.005
Ian is one of our fields leading
philosophers of perception.

00:01:04.005 --> 00:01:06.380
He works on many, many different topics

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.240
with a special focus on
the perception of time,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.530
visual adaptation, blindsight,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.692
object representations,

00:01:12.692 --> 00:01:15.217
many different aspects of visual awareness

00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:16.334
and the lack thereof.

00:01:16.334 --> 00:01:19.760
And he also does some
intriguing secret work

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.760
on silences and shadows,
but that work is classified.

00:01:24.824 --> 00:01:27.127
He's won all sorts of
prizes for this work.

00:01:27.127 --> 00:01:30.000
His work has been selected for

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.600
the Philosopher's Annual which selects

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.650
the 10 best philosophy papers each year.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.351
He's won a Philip Leverhulme Prize.

00:01:37.351 --> 00:01:41.440
And I think he actually
specializes in academic awards,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.378
which have many, many syllables.

00:01:43.378 --> 00:01:45.930
He has won the William James Prize

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.100
for Contributions to the
Scientific Study of Consciousness

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.300
from the Association
for the Scientific Study

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.268
of Consciousness.

00:01:52.268 --> 00:01:53.176
- [Man] Who else.

00:01:53.176 --> 00:01:55.340
- And last year he won

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.726
the Phi Beta Kappa Society

00:01:56.726 --> 00:01:59.020
and American Philosophical Association

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.690
Lebowitz Prize for
Philosophical Achievement

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.013
and Contributions.

00:02:03.013 --> 00:02:06.806
This last one was
co-awarded with Ned Block.

00:02:06.806 --> 00:02:09.860
And I think usually when
prizes in our fields

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.870
are co-awarded that means that
a committee couldn't make up

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.970
their mind or maybe it was a team.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:15.940
But as far as I can tell,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.999
you got that prize with Ned
for just arguing with him

00:02:18.999 --> 00:02:22.613
in print about everything
and really improving

00:02:22.613 --> 00:02:25.810
our field in the process.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.020
Ian is a model not just
of how to make substantive

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.180
philosophical contributions,

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.770
but how to have a dispute in our field

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.578
in a like super productive,
award-winning way.

00:02:34.578 --> 00:02:37.782
In closing, I wanna suggest that Ian

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:41.702
is justifiably renowned in all these ways,

00:02:41.702 --> 00:02:44.343
not just for his
substantive contributions,

00:02:44.343 --> 00:02:47.200
but for the way that
he's doing the philosophy

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.283
of perception, which is pretty exciting.

00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:53.240
This Schulman Series is
about the intersection

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.868
between the sciences and the humanities.

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:58.680
And of course there's been
quite a lot of such interaction

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.440
generally between
philosophy and psychology.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.136
But I think there is a sort
of dark secret in that,

00:03:05.136 --> 00:03:08.265
which is that there's still
quite an asymmetry in one way,

00:03:08.265 --> 00:03:11.925
which is that increasingly
today there are,

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:14.832
I think a great many philosophers
who are paying close,

00:03:14.832 --> 00:03:17.530
careful attention to what's going on

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.811
in the psychological literature

00:03:18.811 --> 00:03:22.065
but there are really
far fewer psychologists

00:03:22.065 --> 00:03:23.960
who are paying close, careful attention

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.189
to what's going on in the
philosophical literature.

00:03:26.189 --> 00:03:29.040
And Ian really breaks this mold.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.945
His work is of course, very
well sighted in philosophy,

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:33.406
which is not so unusual

00:03:33.406 --> 00:03:36.290
but what's more special is that his work

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.120
is increasingly making a
real difference in racking

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.989
up all sorts of citations in
psychology and neuroscience.

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:46.620
And it's really because
he's doing this work

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.908
in a different way, as a vision scientist,

00:03:48.908 --> 00:03:52.360
it's sort of easy to have
a love, hate relationship

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.280
with the philosophy of perception.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.157
And that's in part because so
much philosophy of perception

00:03:57.157 --> 00:03:59.283
has been done by people who know about

00:03:59.283 --> 00:04:02.725
the Muller-Lyer illusion
and that gorilla experiment.

00:04:02.725 --> 00:04:05.250
And that's basically all it takes.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.210
In fact, one of those, you
could write a book on it

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.422
as far as I can tell,

00:04:08.422 --> 00:04:12.251
but Ian in stark contrast has
been really paying attention

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:15.215
to the nitty gritty of all of this work.

00:04:15.215 --> 00:04:17.762
I'm pretty sure he knows
parts of our field better

00:04:17.762 --> 00:04:20.590
than those of us who we're actually

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:21.924
in the trenches doing this work.

00:04:21.924 --> 00:04:25.470
And most importantly, he's
doing philosophical work,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.829
which shows how those
details can really matter

00:04:26.829 --> 00:04:29.360
as I think we'll hear about today,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.878
please welcome Ian Phillips.

00:04:30.878 --> 00:04:34.045
(audience applauding)

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.680
- Okay, thank you so much Brian

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.290
for that incredibly kind introduction.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:41.300
It's over honor to be here.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.007
Thank you for coming in
this gorgeous weather.

00:04:43.007 --> 00:04:47.810
So today I want to begin with a passage

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.634
from the philosopher, Andy Clark,

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:52.091
in which he gives voice to
what he takes to be naive

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:55.922
or intuitive, though in
Clark's view completely

00:04:55.922 --> 00:04:58.290
false vision of how our
mental lives unfold.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.415
So this is what Clark says.

00:05:01.415 --> 00:05:04.350
"Our human mental life looks to involve

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.576
a seamless unfolding of
perception, action, and experience,

00:05:08.576 --> 00:05:11.700
a golden braid in which
each element twines

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.205
intimately with the rest.

00:05:13.205 --> 00:05:14.869
We see the very world we act in

00:05:14.869 --> 00:05:17.669
and we act in the very world we see,

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:20.170
but more than this visual
experience presents us

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:22.480
with the world in a
way apt for the control

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.117
and find guidance of action."

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.500
So I think there are a
kind of three core ideas

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.300
here in the Clark.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.040
The first is just the all perception

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.770
is conscious perception, okay?

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.510
That being the most intimate entwining

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.623
of perception and experience, so to see,

00:05:36.623 --> 00:05:39.130
or to hear and so forth, is just

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:40.700
to perceptually experience the world.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.020
Sand the second thought
is that if you like

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.440
all worldly action guidance is perceptual.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.340
So insofar as the world informs you

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.081
as an agent in the course
of engaging in some action,

00:05:53.081 --> 00:05:56.426
it informs us exclusively
through perceptual channels.

00:05:56.426 --> 00:05:59.930
And the final thought
is something like this,

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:02.200
that all perception is
available for action guidance.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.730
So that's a kind of
minimal access condition

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:09.140
if you like on perceptual experience.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.900
Okay, so we see the world we act in,

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.313
we act in the world we see.

00:06:13.335 --> 00:06:15.408
Now anyone who's at all familiar with

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:19.850
the cognitive science of the
last 50 years will know that

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.110
it has uncovered what
many regard as repeated

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:28.030
and decisive counter
examples to these ideas.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.332
Okay, so grounds for unbraiding this braid

00:06:31.332 --> 00:06:33.629
in particular working
cognitive neuropsychology

00:06:33.629 --> 00:06:36.663
has witnessed apparently
striking dissociation

00:06:36.663 --> 00:06:39.158
of action and awareness and
conditions like blindsight,

00:06:39.158 --> 00:06:42.049
unilateral neglect and visual form agnosia

00:06:42.049 --> 00:06:45.780
and psychophysical work
with neurotypical subjects

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.120
has uncovered extensive evidence

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.388
of visual processing in
the absence of awareness.

00:06:50.388 --> 00:06:52.799
So most notably studies
of subliminal priming,

00:06:52.799 --> 00:06:55.880
and also in the domain of
visually guided action.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.646
And over the last several years,

00:06:58.646 --> 00:07:02.015
I've pressed really a dilemma against

00:07:02.015 --> 00:07:05.555
the idea that perception and
consciousness dissociate,

00:07:05.555 --> 00:07:10.555
which really divides
over a simple question.

00:07:12.394 --> 00:07:13.830
So take any case where
you've allegedly got

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.948
some coming apart of perception
experience or action,

00:07:16.948 --> 00:07:20.610
how did you measure whether experience

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:21.443
was really there or not?

00:07:21.443 --> 00:07:24.271
How did you establish
the absence of awareness?

00:07:24.271 --> 00:07:27.700
So there are two broad categories
of things you might do.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.410
You might use so-called
subjective measures,

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.210
or you might use objective measures.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.550
So let's take the first horn.

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:37.270
So if you were use so-called
subjective measures

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:39.650
to assess awareness, we
just simply asked subjects,

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.110
did you see anything or not?

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:42.565
Or were you conscious of anything or not?

00:07:42.565 --> 00:07:44.856
Then a notorious concern arises

00:07:44.856 --> 00:07:47.506
and it's often called the
problem with the criterion.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:52.240
It goes back to early
signal detection theorists

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.340
and as it manifests in
this particular context,

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.040
it's just the concern that
subjects might underestimate

00:07:58.040 --> 00:07:59.390
their conscious experience.

00:08:00.248 --> 00:08:01.890
And so what I wanna do
now in the first sort

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.970
of substantial chunk of
the talk is illustrate

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:07.370
that concern with respect
to a particular case,

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:10.023
namely the case of blindsight
that Brian mentioned.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.158
Okay, so back in the 70s, researchers

00:08:14.158 --> 00:08:18.550
most notably Larry Weiskrantz
at Oxford began extensive

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:20.680
investigation of a really
astonishing condition,

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.445
which remains the most discussed
neuropsychological case

00:08:23.445 --> 00:08:25.197
in both the contemporary science

00:08:25.197 --> 00:08:27.380
and philosophy of consciousness.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:28.890
And this is work if you're interested,

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:31.010
that's wonderfully summarized
in the third edition

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.460
of this landmark book
"Blindsight" by Weiskrantz.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:35.940
As I'm sure many of you know,

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:38.161
blindsight is a condition
caused by destruction

00:08:38.161 --> 00:08:42.370
of an are of the brain
called primary visual cortex

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.120
it's now at the back of the brain,

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.707
which is kind of the first port of call

00:08:45.707 --> 00:08:48.870
or the first cortical port
of call of visual signals

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.040
from the eyes and here
you can see two scans

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:54.570
of two patients, the most extensively

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.370
investigated patient GY,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.110
who as a child had a road
traffic accident leading

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.500
to a lesion of almost sort of

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:06.242
of V1 primary visual cortex
in his left hemisphere here,

00:09:06.242 --> 00:09:10.420
and then a fascinating, but
much less explored patient TN

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:11.941
who had the misfortune
of having two strokes,

00:09:11.941 --> 00:09:13.980
roughly 30 days apart.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:18.300
And so lost actually more than
just primary visual cortex,

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.339
but primary visual
cortex on first one side

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:22.190
and then the other side of his brain.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.093
So he has bilateral blindsight.

00:09:25.362 --> 00:09:27.400
Okay, and because of the organization

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.024
of the primary visual system,

00:09:29.024 --> 00:09:32.205
the damage to a region of V1 leads to

00:09:32.205 --> 00:09:36.890
so called contra-lateral,
opposites side field deficits.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.707
So patient GY here with a left lesion

00:09:39.707 --> 00:09:44.123
has severe deficits with
perception on the right.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.710
So given that damage,
it's not that surprising

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.600
you might think that
subjects with blindsight

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:55.120
with this such lesions say they're blind

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:56.250
in the corresponding areas.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.606
So if you ask GY, as he
is asked in an interview,

00:09:58.606 --> 00:10:00.812
what happened to him, he just says,

00:10:00.812 --> 00:10:02.539
well, I've lost all
the vision to my right.

00:10:02.539 --> 00:10:04.864
And in the clinic that's confirmed

00:10:04.864 --> 00:10:08.360
by what's called
perimetry, probably an exam

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:09.947
that many of you have had yourselves

00:10:09.947 --> 00:10:13.144
where the clinician just
presents in this case,

00:10:13.144 --> 00:10:18.144
bright, high contrast white
stimuli at various locations

00:10:19.427 --> 00:10:22.610
in that blind right visual field.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:25.879
And what you're seeing
here in this kind of plot,

00:10:25.879 --> 00:10:29.090
these white circles along
the vertical meridian

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:32.250
and at the center here, are
case is where GY has said yes,

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:33.600
by a button press.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.130
I saw the high contrast stimulus,

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:39.620
but these black radio
cases where he said no.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.737
So it looks to all purposes
that he's just blind

00:10:42.737 --> 00:10:46.073
in the vast majority of
his right visual field.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.558
But of course that's not the whole story.

00:10:48.558 --> 00:10:50.800
There's a huge amount of work on patients

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.990
like GY establishing that there's actually

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.580
quite extensive visual function

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:57.150
that remains after this damage.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.520
So does evidence of his capacity

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.059
to discriminate motion in particular left,

00:11:02.059 --> 00:11:04.158
right or horizontal vertical motion

00:11:04.158 --> 00:11:07.696
with another patient the studies that show

00:11:07.696 --> 00:11:10.492
he's able to discriminate
the orientation of a line

00:11:10.492 --> 00:11:12.293
or an X from an O,

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.980
his work on effective
blindsight showing that GY

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:18.420
can distinguish between a happy

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.650
and sad emotionally expressed emotions,

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.953
a facially expressed expressed emotions.

00:11:22.953 --> 00:11:25.338
And again, with GY that he can detect

00:11:25.338 --> 00:11:29.073
simple high contrast
stimuli in his blind field.

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:33.120
So I mentioned before patient TN,

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:35.290
and this is perhaps the
most dramatic example

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.724
of preserve visual function.

00:11:36.724 --> 00:11:40.955
So remember TN has complete
bilateral blindsight.

00:11:40.955 --> 00:11:42.773
So denies being able to see anything

00:11:42.773 --> 00:11:45.610
in a perimetry exam, but this is

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:48.095
a video of him walking down a corridor

00:11:48.095 --> 00:11:52.420
and that's Weiskrantz
closely shadowing him behind.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:55.201
And as you'll see, he's able to navigate

00:11:55.201 --> 00:11:58.060
these kind of bits of office paraphernalia

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:01.590
that have been put in his
path pretty effectively,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:05.200
despite saying, I have
no idea how I did this.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:06.250
I'm completely blind.

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.710
And this kind of performance has led many

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.880
to question kind what's the
point of being conscious

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.967
if you can get around the world like this

00:12:13.967 --> 00:12:17.753
without being conscious,
then why are we conscious?

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:21.558
Okay, so whatever exactly you think

00:12:21.558 --> 00:12:26.558
an extremely common view is
that blindside is revolutionary.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.810
So here's Weiskrantz in retrospect

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:32.890
describing how back in the 70s,

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.170
which is immediately obvious
to him and his colleagues

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:37.830
that the phenomenon of
successful performance

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.550
without awareness across a
startling new broad range

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.790
of visual tasks just must have a bearing

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:45.520
on the neural and philosophical
aspects of consciousness

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:49.050
and here's a philosopher Paul
Churchland, actually writing

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.526
in the context of Weiskrantz's work of how

00:12:51.526 --> 00:12:54.040
the revolutionary blindsight result

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.880
just knocked the stuffing
out of the obvious assumption

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.030
that awareness of a signal is necessary

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.730
for an intentional
response to that signal.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:03.630
And that's just part of
the golden braid conception

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:05.490
that we need to be aware of things

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:09.110
if we're going to act in
response to them in the world.

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:11.300
So, first question, does blindsight show

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:13.663
that the golden braid is mistaken?

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:19.040
Well, in fact, there's a simple
and powerful alternative way

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:20.567
of thinking about blindsight,

00:13:20.567 --> 00:13:23.145
but one I think that's
been unjustly neglected.

00:13:23.145 --> 00:13:26.656
And so to approach this
kind of alternative picture,

00:13:26.656 --> 00:13:30.618
notice first that the
tasks used to establish

00:13:30.618 --> 00:13:32.808
the absence of awareness in blindsight

00:13:32.808 --> 00:13:34.397
are subjective measures.

00:13:34.397 --> 00:13:37.427
So in the perimetry
exam I mentioned before

00:13:37.427 --> 00:13:39.620
GY's just being asked, you know,

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.150
did you see anything or not

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.620
following the presentation of a stimulus?

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.970
And that kind of question is subject

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.833
to what is known as
conservative response bias.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:53.510
So let me just illustrate that

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:55.320
and I want you to think of yourself

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:57.735
as a blindsight patient like GY,

00:13:57.735 --> 00:14:02.735
and so you have by any objective measure,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.520
extremely degraded vision in
your right hand visual field.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:07.730
Okay, so you have pretty normal vision

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:08.864
on your left hand field.

00:14:08.864 --> 00:14:10.719
That's how you get around
the world normally,

00:14:10.719 --> 00:14:13.560
but your right hand field,
you don't see colors,

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.137
you don't really see visual form at all.

00:14:15.137 --> 00:14:17.476
You don't really see all
objects in any real sense.

00:14:17.476 --> 00:14:19.152
Maybe you just have a sense somehow

00:14:19.152 --> 00:14:21.570
I mean, I'm not saying this
it's conscious at this point,

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:26.150
but just the objective
measures are, you know,

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:28.855
of you being able to detect
extremely high contrast

00:14:28.855 --> 00:14:32.223
or extremely kind of salient stimuli only.

00:14:33.358 --> 00:14:35.890
So the experiment to ask you after

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:37.137
the presentation is some stimulus,

00:14:37.137 --> 00:14:39.970
did you see anything on that trial?

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.700
And what are you supposed
to make of that question?

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:44.590
You might think maybe this is sort

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.730
of somewhat philosophical kind of question

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.570
and philosophers do
not agree on the answer

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:49.648
to this question.

00:14:49.648 --> 00:14:51.630
What does it take to see something?

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.050
What does it mean to see something?

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:54.910
I mean, that's just
being handed over to me

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:56.249
to make a decision about that.

00:14:56.249 --> 00:14:59.600
How sure do I have to be that
what has just happened to me

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:00.790
counts as a case of seeing?

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:01.760
How sure do I have to be that there

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:03.119
was a stimulus there to say yes and so on?

00:15:03.119 --> 00:15:06.670
Maybe you think something like this,

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:09.440
well, the experience
I had was just nothing

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:11.440
like seeing something
in my I sighted field,

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.420
so quotes, normal seeing.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.130
So I should probably say no.

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:16.100
Or you might think, well,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.010
I had some really vague awareness,

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:20.140
but I wouldn't say I
saw anything specific.

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:22.237
So I guess I'll say no.

00:15:22.237 --> 00:15:24.640
Or you might think I'm not sure whether

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:25.500
I saw something or not.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:26.750
So I maybe I'll play it safe

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:27.893
and I'll say no.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.371
Okay, so for all of those
reasons, and may the others,

00:15:31.371 --> 00:15:36.371
you might think a negative
response in an exam

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.430
like this needn't be taken as a guarantee

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:45.070
that there was absolutely no awareness

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.560
because there's this other factor, namely,

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.790
all that decisional
thinking that goes on after

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:52.040
your experience that we
need to take into account.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:55.530
Okay, so that's the tasks that
I used to assess awareness.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:56.650
What about the tasks that I used

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:59.223
to assess residual visual capacity?

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:02.180
Well, these are typically
so-called forced choice

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:04.362
or maybe forced response tasks.

00:16:04.362 --> 00:16:07.660
And these are effectively, at
least the forced choice tasks

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:09.750
effectively bias free.

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:11.938
So let me say what I mean by that.

00:16:11.938 --> 00:16:14.230
So in a forced choice
task you're presented

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.017
with in a two alternative case,

00:16:16.017 --> 00:16:18.780
two temporal spatial intervals.

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:21.710
So there's gonna be a stimulus
in just one of those two

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:23.579
intervals and you have to choose which.

00:16:23.579 --> 00:16:26.529
And so here, I mean I have no idea

00:16:26.529 --> 00:16:28.430
how these slides appear to you

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.188
and whether it's completely obvious to you

00:16:30.188 --> 00:16:32.531
that there's a patch in
one and not in the other,

00:16:32.531 --> 00:16:37.531
but suppose they were in
presented to you on their own.

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:39.410
And you might think,

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:42.030
I don't think I can see
anything in either of those.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.370
So each interval presented
on its own is one

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:48.193
such that you would say,
no, I don't see anything,

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:51.650
but you don't have that
option here of saying,

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:52.590
no, there's nothing there,

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:53.580
or I don't see anything.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.717
You have to choose which of
these, the stimulus is in.

00:16:55.717 --> 00:16:58.503
And you know there is a
stimulus in one of them.

00:16:58.503 --> 00:17:00.600
So what do you do when you naturally pick

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:02.430
whichever interval it
is that's most likely

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.010
to contain a stimulus.

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.810
Perhaps you might think
that which just gave

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:08.980
you that dimmest flicker
or hint of awareness

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:10.530
of there being something there.

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:16.400
Okay, so you might think
that, but you have to choose.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.010
So you think maybe there
was something faint

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:19.660
in the first and you go for that.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:23.206
So I hope it's clear, even
from this brief presentation

00:17:23.206 --> 00:17:25.330
that these forced choice tasks are kind

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:27.450
of a great tool for a psycho physicist

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:30.077
to ascertain a subject
sensitivity independent

00:17:30.077 --> 00:17:33.710
of their bias, because bias
doesn't really factor into this,

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:35.140
unless you have a particular preference

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:37.300
for interval ones over interval twos

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.100
there's no question of
conservative criterion coming in.

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:43.395
Okay, so that kind of framework sets up

00:17:43.395 --> 00:17:46.077
the thought that actually all
the data concerning blindsight

00:17:46.077 --> 00:17:48.410
is consistent with a picture on which

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:51.314
what the brain damage
has done is to severely

00:17:51.314 --> 00:17:52.969
and maybe qualitatively degrade

00:17:52.969 --> 00:17:56.090
the subject's vision in their blind field,

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:57.790
perhaps to the point
where it's just too weak

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:00.588
or degraded or qualitatively
different to count to them

00:18:00.588 --> 00:18:04.270
as seeing, as I say,
there's no color, no form,

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:06.030
no objects and so on.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:07.622
But nonetheless, there is
a conscious signal there,

00:18:07.622 --> 00:18:10.260
which is sufficient to
perform above chance

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:12.140
when you force the
subject to make a decision

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:13.393
between two alternatives.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:17.249
So I can't give a full defense
of that viewpoint here,

00:18:17.249 --> 00:18:20.354
but I just want to give you three sort of,

00:18:20.354 --> 00:18:23.790
almost tantalizing teaser bits of evidence

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:26.803
that make me think this
hypothesis is on the right lines.

00:18:26.803 --> 00:18:31.803
So one important thing to notice
is that blindsight patients

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:34.520
do report awareness,
especially when the question

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.185
is changed subtly and what you think,

00:18:37.185 --> 00:18:39.440
what I think maybe the question changes

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:43.450
about is the question
change elicits a different,

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:45.100
more liberal criterion in the subject.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.460
So they're more prepared to say yes

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:48.130
to this question than the others.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:50.450
So here I mentioned slight misleadingly

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:53.288
that this was the perimetry
plot from this paper,

00:18:53.288 --> 00:18:55.440
and it is the classic perimetry plot

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:56.970
in all the early papers on blindsight.

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:59.209
That's, you know, the kind
of plot you see for GY,

00:18:59.209 --> 00:19:01.610
but these experimentalists also asked

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:04.200
a different question with
just the same stimuli

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:05.680
in a second block.

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:06.937
So that different question was just press

00:19:06.937 --> 00:19:08.770
when you're aware of something.

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.490
So we had press when you see something,

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:12.530
nothing, press when
you're aware of something

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:15.860
and suddenly you get all
these positive responses.

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:18.353
Okay, so there's clearly
some kind of awareness

00:19:18.353 --> 00:19:22.403
in much of the visual field
if you trust these answers.

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:25.563
Second kind of bit of evidence,

00:19:25.563 --> 00:19:29.080
usually blindsight patients
in these experiments

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:34.020
are given a binary choice
between yes and no responses.

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.920
They can either say yes, I saw
it, or no, I didn't see it.

00:19:38.014 --> 00:19:40.203
But what happens when we
give a blindsight subject

00:19:40.203 --> 00:19:42.515
a more fine grain response scale?

00:19:42.515 --> 00:19:45.877
So this is a study done
by Mazzi and colleagues

00:19:45.877 --> 00:19:48.454
with a different patient,
a woman called SL.

00:19:48.454 --> 00:19:51.490
And what they did was
just give her a bunch

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:54.340
of different discrimination
tasks, like orientation color,

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:55.534
and so on discrimination tasks.

00:19:55.534 --> 00:19:57.397
And after every trial asked her,

00:19:57.397 --> 00:20:00.960
did you do that task
by seeing the stimulus

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:02.840
or were you just guessing?

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.303
So a standard yes, no measure.

00:20:05.303 --> 00:20:06.904
And what they found was that in,

00:20:06.904 --> 00:20:10.150
the black bars indicate,
I was just guessing.

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:13.044
So no responses, that only
in one of the cases here

00:20:13.044 --> 00:20:16.620
did she acknowledge that
she was seeing anything

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:20.250
and that that was driving her responses.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:21.390
But then again, they did exactly

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:24.051
the same discrimination
tasks, but affording her

00:20:24.051 --> 00:20:27.530
the luxury of being able to
respond in four different ways.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.485
So she could say I had no
experience, I had weak experience,

00:20:30.485 --> 00:20:35.300
I had almost clear experience,
or I had clear experience,

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:36.607
and this is what happened.

00:20:36.607 --> 00:20:39.900
It turned out that the black bars,

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:41.973
which are indicating guess responses,

00:20:41.973 --> 00:20:45.850
so, no, I didn't have any
experiences, actually look

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:49.176
like they comprised
zero, so no experience,

00:20:49.176 --> 00:20:53.857
but also this dark gray,
I had weak experience.

00:20:53.857 --> 00:20:56.310
And similarly the yes, I saw something,

00:20:56.310 --> 00:21:01.310
corresponds here to
almost clear experience.

00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:03.580
So it looks like what the
subject has been doing

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:07.930
with the yes, no is using
no to mean none or weak

00:21:07.930 --> 00:21:12.160
and the yes, to mean
clear or almost clear.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.150
And it's a little hard
to tell too much from

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.940
this particular study
because the performance

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:17.140
is quite low across the board,

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:19.385
but there was no evidence
of above chance performance,

00:21:19.385 --> 00:21:21.862
just looking at the
cases where no experience

00:21:21.862 --> 00:21:25.170
at all was reported that had to at least

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:26.300
be weak experience before

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:28.483
reported before or one
got any performance.

00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:33.305
Okay, final bit of teaser evidence.

00:21:33.305 --> 00:21:38.305
This is from a study by
Zeki and FFytche of GY.

00:21:38.825 --> 00:21:41.404
And they gave him a whole
bunch of different kind

00:21:41.404 --> 00:21:43.115
of conditions and in particular,

00:21:43.115 --> 00:21:46.054
in one particular very
low contrast condition,

00:21:46.054 --> 00:21:48.518
he just spontaneously responds.

00:21:48.518 --> 00:21:50.873
I mean, he knows his
experimenters very well,

00:21:50.873 --> 00:21:55.746
you know, hey, my awareness
score in this particular block

00:21:55.746 --> 00:21:58.685
should be quotes minus one or minus two.

00:21:58.685 --> 00:22:01.466
And I think what that really tells you

00:22:01.466 --> 00:22:04.740
is that his zero was not absolute zero

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:07.480
if he's prepared to give minus
one or minus two responses.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:09.907
Okay so in general, I hope
that gives you some sense

00:22:09.907 --> 00:22:11.020
(audience laughing)

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:14.130
that the judgments of
awareness are one should

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:16.164
be suspicious about and
it turns out that they,

00:22:16.164 --> 00:22:18.980
there is awareness
there if you really find

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.330
the right way to at it.

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:22.890
And I think there's a case to
be made that that correlates

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:24.815
quite well with performance also.

00:22:24.815 --> 00:22:27.910
But if you want a really
systematic and sustained defense

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:30.575
of that view against lots of
psychophysical objections,

00:22:30.575 --> 00:22:33.653
then let me advertise this paper.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:37.610
Okay, whatever one thinks of blindsight,

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:39.430
the residual vision is
really very limited.

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:41.770
So, you know, there
are sometimes anecdotes

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:44.774
about how a blindsighted
patient wouldn't reach out

00:22:44.774 --> 00:22:46.270
for a glass of water if they were thirsty,

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:47.800
well that's 'cause they can't see objects.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:49.892
I mean there's no vision capable

00:22:49.892 --> 00:22:53.853
of anything like that kind of richness.

00:22:54.829 --> 00:22:57.960
But in the 80s research is interested

00:22:57.960 --> 00:22:59.620
in a rather different condition

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:01.369
reported evidence of as they put it

00:23:01.369 --> 00:23:04.020
unconscious perception analysis

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:06.640
far more extensive than a dreamt of

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:08.270
in most current theories.

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:09.740
And the most current
theories were precisely

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:12.440
this theories that were being
motivated by blindsight.

00:23:14.195 --> 00:23:15.780
So this is evidence from
a condition called neglect

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:18.993
and neglect is a really complicated

00:23:18.993 --> 00:23:20.715
and kind of fractionated disorder,

00:23:20.715 --> 00:23:23.642
it's associated to much later damage

00:23:23.642 --> 00:23:25.715
in the visual hierarchy.

00:23:25.715 --> 00:23:28.340
So typically it follows a stroke

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:30.470
and it's associated with damage to

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:33.410
kind of right inferior parietal

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:35.520
and temporal parietal regions.

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:38.537
And it's characterized
by a failure to respond,

00:23:38.537 --> 00:23:43.537
or report, or attend to, or
orient to significant stimuli

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:45.000
on the other side of space

00:23:46.323 --> 00:23:48.600
where that's not due to, you know,

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:51.210
you not being able to see
in some elementary senses,

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:54.300
no damage to your eyes or
very early visual areas

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:55.540
nor to being able to respond

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:59.000
in the kind of other end
of things in a motorway.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:01.470
And you often see these kinds
of pictures in textbooks.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:02.828
So this is a picture of a house

00:24:02.828 --> 00:24:05.372
and the patient has just
been asked to copy it

00:24:05.372 --> 00:24:08.420
and you can see they've
just failed to copy out

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:09.483
the left hand side,

00:24:09.483 --> 00:24:11.571
here they've been asked to bisect a line

00:24:11.571 --> 00:24:14.570
and you can see that
they've bisected it too far

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:16.969
to the right as if they're
ignoring this part of the line.

00:24:16.969 --> 00:24:19.279
And this classic line cancellation task

00:24:19.279 --> 00:24:22.680
is a case where the
patient just in free vision

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:26.610
has been asked to strike through
all the lines they can see.

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:28.764
And they've not done that with
the ones on the left at all,

00:24:28.764 --> 00:24:31.664
but they've repeatedly done
it with the ones on the right.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:35.481
Okay, and so here's a
really classic study,

00:24:35.481 --> 00:24:38.586
really a case report rather than a study,

00:24:38.586 --> 00:24:42.180
that it was the beginning of
a much more extensive body

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.059
of work but I just wanna
talk about the simple study,

00:24:44.059 --> 00:24:46.210
partly 'cause it's the one
that's always cited still

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:49.710
and you know, widely
discussed even to this day,

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:51.638
I just read Hackman Lao's
new book on consciousness,

00:24:51.638 --> 00:24:53.150
which is a really interesting book.

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:57.790
And it talks about this
study and it suffices,

00:24:57.790 --> 00:25:00.890
I think to make the basic
point about a lot of the work.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:03.339
So here a patient called
PS with severe neglect

00:25:03.339 --> 00:25:07.800
was shown these two houses just on cards

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.570
and asked the following questions.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:11.520
Are these houses the same or different?

00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:14.060
As she says they're the same.

00:25:15.166 --> 00:25:16.590
Is there anything wrong with either card?

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:17.423
Nope.

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:20.870
Okay, but which house would
you prefer to live in?

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:23.173
And understandably she says,
that's a silly question,

00:25:23.173 --> 00:25:25.730
but when forced to make a response,

00:25:25.730 --> 00:25:27.240
she chooses there's none burning one

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:29.250
on nine of the 11 trials.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.620
Kind of that's why it's not a study,

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:32.990
but just the nine of the 11 trials.

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:34.700
But you know, this is someone who's

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:36.050
just had a stroke probably.

00:25:37.445 --> 00:25:40.390
Okay what the, do the authors say,

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.130
well, they say that these
preference judgments

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.200
clearly indicate covert knowledge

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.113
without conscious perceptual awareness,

00:25:46.113 --> 00:25:49.300
showing unconscious perception
analysis far more extensive,

00:25:49.300 --> 00:25:51.917
as I said than is dreamt
of in our current theories

00:25:51.917 --> 00:25:53.840
and so what are they thinking,

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:54.790
well they're presumably thinking

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:56.057
that she's seeing these flames,

00:25:56.057 --> 00:25:59.020
you can see this as
house is a burning house.

00:25:59.020 --> 00:26:01.010
That's what's driving
her preference judgment,

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:02.650
but that can't be
conscious 'cause otherwise

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:04.085
she would've said they were different

00:26:04.085 --> 00:26:06.973
or something was wrong
with the two houses.

00:26:08.539 --> 00:26:10.100
The problem here again though,

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:11.450
is that these questions are very,

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:13.640
very different questions
to be asking someone.

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:15.631
If you ask a question are two
things the same or different,

00:26:15.631 --> 00:26:17.839
you need yourself to make up your mind

00:26:17.839 --> 00:26:19.500
like, well how different do two things

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:21.200
have to be before I
count them is different.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:26.200
You know, how much difference
do there have to be?

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:29.400
Similarly if you're asked the question,

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:30.647
is there anything wrong with either card,

00:26:30.647 --> 00:26:32.070
you have to make up your mind,

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:33.900
well, how much has to be wrong before

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:34.850
I say something is wrong?

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:36.050
What is it to be?

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:38.432
What is it for something
to be wrong with something?

00:26:38.432 --> 00:26:42.017
Okay, so these are questions
that are both biased

00:26:42.017 --> 00:26:45.663
in the sense that subjects show
a tendency to say the same,

00:26:45.663 --> 00:26:49.294
even when there are visible
differences between stimuli.

00:26:49.294 --> 00:26:52.190
So that's would be a kind
of way of being conservative

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:54.576
towards making different judgments.

00:26:54.576 --> 00:26:57.379
And again, you might expect
people to be conservative

00:26:57.379 --> 00:26:59.569
in saying something was wrong because

00:26:59.569 --> 00:27:02.550
they might be hesitant to do that.

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:04.354
In contrast, this is a slightly unusual,

00:27:04.354 --> 00:27:05.740
but it's a forced choice question.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:07.383
You have to pick one of these two houses.

00:27:07.383 --> 00:27:09.910
So you don't have the
option of saying, well,

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:11.650
I don't really care.

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:12.483
I mean, she does say that,

00:27:12.483 --> 00:27:14.194
but then she's forced to guess.

00:27:14.194 --> 00:27:19.194
So once we see that the
pattern of responses here

00:27:19.697 --> 00:27:21.917
and in future experiments
is just fully consistent

00:27:21.917 --> 00:27:25.040
with a picture on which
what neglect has led to

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.100
is some kind of degraded
conscious awareness

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:28.580
of the left hand side of this object

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:30.280
or the left hand side of space.

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:33.160
Maybe just something being a
bit off with one of the houses

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:35.000
or something being
slightly smudgy over there

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:36.813
or an asymmetry in the stimulus.

00:27:36.813 --> 00:27:40.827
And that's not sufficient
to drive a different

00:27:40.827 --> 00:27:45.640
or yes response, but it is to bias her,

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:47.480
to lead her to make this
preference judgment.

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:49.480
So I mean, maybe it's a bit like this.

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:51.380
I don't know whether you can see anything

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:52.870
in the left hand side of space there,

00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:55.120
but that's just this
kind of slight smudging.

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:02.140
Okay, so let's go back to our dilemma.

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:04.140
So that's subjective
measures and how they crop up

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:05.891
into kind of critical cases

00:28:05.891 --> 00:28:08.670
but what if we adopt a more
rigorous objective test

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:10.390
of awareness and insists that a stimulus

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:11.830
just has to be completely undetectable

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:13.976
of to count as unconscious

00:28:13.976 --> 00:28:16.676
that would rule out blindsight and neglect

00:28:16.676 --> 00:28:18.700
as cases of unconscious perception

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:19.974
or at least clear cases of such,

00:28:19.974 --> 00:28:23.803
but it would also overcome this
problem with the criterion.

00:28:26.410 --> 00:28:28.190
Okay, so if we do that,

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:29.840
then I think a different issue arises.

00:28:29.840 --> 00:28:31.931
And the key point here is
if the subject cannot detect

00:28:31.931 --> 00:28:34.360
the stimulus, we need
some other way of showing

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:35.288
that they've seen it.

00:28:35.288 --> 00:28:38.366
And a standard way of doing that would be

00:28:38.366 --> 00:28:41.381
to do a priming study and
show some priming effects.

00:28:41.381 --> 00:28:44.989
So we might present the
subject with this arrow here,

00:28:44.989 --> 00:28:47.410
mask it shortly afterwards
and in such a way

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:49.129
that perhaps the prime is undetectable

00:28:49.129 --> 00:28:52.703
and then follow it up with a
consciously perceived target

00:28:52.703 --> 00:28:55.690
to which we ask the subject
to respond in some way.

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:57.670
So maybe they just press left or right

00:28:57.670 --> 00:28:59.142
and it turns out that
their right responses

00:28:59.142 --> 00:29:02.890
are marginally faster when
a congruent right prime

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:05.363
has been proceeded, has proceeded them.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.100
Okay, let's assume we do get such effects.

00:29:10.780 --> 00:29:12.582
The issue though here is that
the priming effect itself

00:29:12.582 --> 00:29:14.168
can be understood simply in terms

00:29:14.168 --> 00:29:16.130
of the automatic activation of

00:29:16.130 --> 00:29:18.610
some subsystem of the individual.

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:20.910
So maybe their visual system
or maybe their visual motor

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:23.445
system can think of
this primers, you know,

00:29:23.445 --> 00:29:26.218
activating representations
in your early visual areas

00:29:26.218 --> 00:29:30.898
that since activated
will lead to this target

00:29:30.898 --> 00:29:34.420
being perceived slightly
faster if they're congruent

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:35.680
as opposed to incongruent

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:38.120
or maybe the motor response
has been activated.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:39.850
And so you're slightly
faster to fire it off

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:41.343
when you see this target.

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.688
But perception isn't a state
of your primary visual cortex

00:29:45.688 --> 00:29:48.210
or of your supplementary motor area

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:50.142
or any of these parts of your brain nor

00:29:50.142 --> 00:29:53.160
even of a cognitive subsystem of yours.

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:55.210
It's a state that you are
in, you are the perceiver,

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:56.710
you perceive things.

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:58.620
So we can't just conclude from an effect

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:00.980
that shows an effect on a sub system

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:03.011
that you yourself have
perceived something.

00:30:03.011 --> 00:30:05.232
So I call this a problem of attribution

00:30:05.232 --> 00:30:08.943
and you know, it's like,

00:30:08.943 --> 00:30:10.763
I think an extremely difficult question

00:30:10.763 --> 00:30:12.560
as to how we should solve this problem.

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:15.083
Like what are the marks
of the individual level?

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:19.677
But I kind of widely agreed mark,

00:30:19.677 --> 00:30:22.870
and I say mark advisedly because
know many people would say

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:24.843
it wasn't necessary or sufficient,

00:30:26.217 --> 00:30:28.920
but just a good guide
of the individual level

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:31.250
is if a state or an
episode or representation

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:33.310
is directly available to guide action.

00:30:33.310 --> 00:30:35.986
So if a subject can
use that representation

00:30:35.986 --> 00:30:39.020
to make some choice or
to guide their action

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:42.380
in some purpose of way,
then we should think

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:44.580
of it as an individual
level representation.

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:47.713
And these primes aren't
available to do that.

00:30:47.713 --> 00:30:51.733
So the action is guided
by this target here.

00:30:51.733 --> 00:30:55.540
These simply modulate the
way you end up responding

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:57.713
to this in some modest way.

00:30:59.233 --> 00:31:02.548
But as a result of this kind
of dilemma in the setup,

00:31:02.548 --> 00:31:04.640
a lot of the debate and
Brian mentioned Ned.

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:06.460
So, you know, here are
some of the many things

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:08.150
I've written in debate with Ned

00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:10.090
about these issues has
really come to focus

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:11.960
on this issue of
unconscious action guidance.

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:15.740
So the reason for that
is that if we could find

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:17.960
compelling evidence of
unconscious action guidance,

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:20.390
that gets around the
problem of attribution

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:23.440
and so does provide a case of kind of

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:25.853
a clear counter example
to the golden braid.

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:28.187
And these are some other
papers which again,

00:31:28.187 --> 00:31:31.110
by Josh Shepard and Myrto
Mylopoulos and Jake Berger,

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:33.540
which again, kind of
press the same thought.

00:31:33.540 --> 00:31:34.750
So in the last part of the talk,

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:37.000
I want to talk about a
couple of these cases.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.640
Okay, so in the background here is

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.530
a canonical framework for thinking

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:52.530
about visual and motor
action due to Mel Goodale

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:55.187
And David Milner here,
called the so-called

00:31:55.187 --> 00:31:57.340
two visual system hypothesis

00:31:57.340 --> 00:31:59.474
and very, very roughly the hypothesis says

00:31:59.474 --> 00:32:04.474
that when visual signal goes
on from primary visual cortex,

00:32:04.598 --> 00:32:07.150
it proceeds into anatomically

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:09.340
and functionally separate streams.

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:11.155
Okay, the first pathway,

00:32:11.155 --> 00:32:13.898
which is a kind of eventual pathway here

00:32:13.898 --> 00:32:17.730
Milner and Goodale dubbed
vision for perception,

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:20.690
since in their view, it is
responsible for the construction

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:23.556
of conscious representations
of our environments,

00:32:23.556 --> 00:32:26.040
representation which
gets thought in memory

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:28.930
and are then used for, you
know, making judgments,

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:30.273
recognizing, categorizing,

00:32:31.644 --> 00:32:33.352
and also thinking about the world

00:32:33.352 --> 00:32:34.410
and planning future actions

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:37.110
and a second pathway here
that Milner and Goodale

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:38.032
called vision for action

00:32:38.032 --> 00:32:41.467
and which is claimed to be responsible

00:32:41.467 --> 00:32:45.300
for the online control of
our visually guided actions.

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:47.203
So reaching and grasping and so on,

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:50.500
and a critical part of
this framework has it

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:52.530
that the information in this dorsal stream

00:32:52.530 --> 00:32:55.400
is completely unconscious, okay?

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:57.600
In principle cannot be
accessed consciously.

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:01.050
There's been hugely
influential in philosophy.

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:02.370
I won't read out these quotations,

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:05.034
but here's Andy Clark,

00:33:05.034 --> 00:33:07.960
the context of his rejection
of the golden braid

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:09.322
is precisely this,

00:33:09.322 --> 00:33:14.322
insisting that the involvement
of this dorsal information

00:33:15.770 --> 00:33:18.450
in our online action control
is just completely immense

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:19.440
and pervasive.

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:21.092
Again raising a question about why we need

00:33:21.092 --> 00:33:23.300
to be conscious at all.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:26.290
Here's Brogaard saying
something very similar arguing

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:27.820
that actually we're never
really consciously aware

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:29.160
of exactly what we're doing

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:31.210
because all of what
we're doing at a moment

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:33.060
is being guided by the dorsal stream.

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:37.373
That's not to say there's not
been push back in philosophy.

00:33:38.684 --> 00:33:41.210
So they're very nice paper by
Josh Shwenkler and Rob Briscoe

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:44.047
from a few years ago saying that

00:33:44.047 --> 00:33:47.110
there's the two visual
assistant hypothesis too crude.

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:49.420
That there's plenty of evidence
that the spatial contents

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:51.810
of visual experience are
eventual stream representations

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:55.506
are in fact involved in
online motor guidance

00:33:55.506 --> 00:33:56.803
and programming.

00:33:57.780 --> 00:34:00.710
But even they are not
interested in objecting

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:03.010
to the idea that there's just
masses of empirical evidence,

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:06.090
that there are unconscious
sources of information

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:09.373
that do significantly
influence our bodily actions.

00:34:13.596 --> 00:34:15.646
Okay, so why think that the
dorsal stream representation

00:34:15.646 --> 00:34:17.093
is unconscious?

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:21.130
I think there are two basic reasons.

00:34:21.130 --> 00:34:24.080
And so here's Josh Shepherd
and Myrto Mylopoulos

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:27.500
asking and answering that
question citing these two sources.

00:34:27.500 --> 00:34:29.990
They say the first source
of evidence involves

00:34:29.990 --> 00:34:31.233
visual and motor appointing tasks.

00:34:31.233 --> 00:34:34.772
And the second involves this
case of visual form agnosia

00:34:34.772 --> 00:34:37.390
in particular patient DF.

00:34:37.390 --> 00:34:38.990
So let me just talk about those.

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:42.290
This is actually really old body of work.

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:44.090
One of the luxuries being
philosopher is that you can,

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:46.880
you know, site work from 50
years ago and that's okay.

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:48.260
But I think it's really
important to go back

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:49.940
to the foundations of these things

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:51.960
and reassess them sometimes.

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:53.170
So this is work that goes back to,

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:55.820
to Bruce Bridgeman in the early 80s,

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:57.970
I guess it kind of came to its full,

00:34:59.603 --> 00:35:04.603
flourishing with Mark Jeannerod here.

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:07.350
Mel Goodale did a bunch of this work.

00:35:07.350 --> 00:35:09.670
There's a huge research program here,

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:11.860
and it's still widely
appealed to by philosophers,

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:14.350
but here are two classic tasks.

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:18.029
So in this task from Goodale,
Pelisso, Prablanc from 86,

00:35:18.029 --> 00:35:21.403
you, the subject will be
sat in front of a target

00:35:21.403 --> 00:35:25.709
and your task is to wait for that target

00:35:25.709 --> 00:35:30.002
to jump to your right and
then to reach out immediately

00:35:30.002 --> 00:35:32.010
and point to it.

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:34.210
So the target's gonna
jump, you point to it.

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:38.248
In 50% of the trials though, the target

00:35:38.248 --> 00:35:40.360
is gonna jump an extra 10%.

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:42.800
You're not told about this,
but this is just the case.

00:35:42.800 --> 00:35:45.797
So it's gonna jump an extra
three to five centimeters

00:35:45.797 --> 00:35:48.040
just as you're saccading,

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:50.390
just as you're moving
your eyes to that target.

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:52.860
So under these conditions,

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:55.180
subjects never notice that
there is any extra jump

00:35:55.180 --> 00:35:57.300
it's time to coincide
with their eye movements.

00:35:57.300 --> 00:36:00.600
So it's kind of hidden, but nonetheless,

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:01.990
they adjust their
trajectory as if the target

00:36:01.990 --> 00:36:04.270
had basically immediately
gone to that location.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.765
So there's just no difference
in the trajectories

00:36:06.765 --> 00:36:08.240
of the jump targets,

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:10.873
as opposed to one that
was sent there originally.

00:36:11.710 --> 00:36:15.090
And on that basis, Goodale
and colleagues say, look,

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:16.540
this is just a clear demonstration that

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:18.610
the neural mechanisms
mediating the perception

00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:20.720
of target position can be dissociated from

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:24.010
those mediating visually
guided reaching movements,

00:36:24.010 --> 00:36:25.990
a dissociation reminiscent of the behavior

00:36:25.990 --> 00:36:27.760
of cortically blind patients.

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:29.090
It's actually repeated,

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:31.980
refrain in this literature
that blindsight is set up

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:34.380
something that constantly
gets referred back to.

00:36:36.499 --> 00:36:37.332
Okay, here's a second experiment,

00:36:37.332 --> 00:36:39.810
very similar by Castiello and colleagues

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:41.060
from Mark Jeannerod's lab.

00:36:41.060 --> 00:36:44.392
You as a subject again, sat
in front of some targets

00:36:44.392 --> 00:36:46.267
this time there are
these translucent dowels

00:36:46.267 --> 00:36:47.920
which can be illuminated

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:52.890
and your task initially
is just to wait for

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:55.910
the dowel to light up and
then reach out towards it.

00:36:55.910 --> 00:37:00.020
And also to make the sound
tah, just to say, tah out loud,

00:37:00.020 --> 00:37:02.133
just a meaningless vocalization,

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:06.880
But in the critical session,

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:09.190
trials all began with the
second dowel being illuminated.

00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:12.102
But then in the minority
in 20% of the trials,

00:37:12.102 --> 00:37:15.350
the light would jump to
the left or to the right.

00:37:15.350 --> 00:37:18.280
Subjects were told this would happen.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:21.660
And in these pertubated trials,

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.640
they were instructed to
just track the shift.

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:26.736
So instead of going to the
middle one, now go left or right,

00:37:26.736 --> 00:37:30.580
And then say tah again, to indicate that

00:37:30.580 --> 00:37:33.300
they'd consciously
experience the visual change

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:34.350
that had taken place.

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:37.659
Okay, so second one goes
on, you say tah immediately,

00:37:37.659 --> 00:37:40.050
then it jumps and then you say tah again

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:41.050
and reach out to it.

00:37:43.210 --> 00:37:48.210
Looking at the motor correction
that occurs in this study,

00:37:48.325 --> 00:37:52.601
the experimentalist established
that the reaction time,

00:37:52.601 --> 00:37:55.022
the time it takes between
the jump happening

00:37:55.022 --> 00:37:57.650
and the hand trajectory changing is

00:37:57.650 --> 00:37:59.630
about a hundred milliseconds,

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:02.160
but the vocal reaction
time was much longer,

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:04.710
nearly 420 milliseconds on average.

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:06.250
So it took nearly, you know,

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:08.890
oh, well over 300 milliseconds for sub,

00:38:08.890 --> 00:38:10.146
between the change in

00:38:10.146 --> 00:38:14.820
the movement and the vocalization to come.

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:16.830
And indeed on some occasions they report

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:19.224
the dissociation was so striking

00:38:19.224 --> 00:38:22.010
that to their own surprise, subjects

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:24.700
were already lifting
the first or third dowel

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:27.893
by the time they were
emitting that vocal response.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:30.100
So what does this show?

00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:33.671
Well, the thought is
that's given in the paper

00:38:33.671 --> 00:38:36.199
is that this extra 300
milliseconds is the time required

00:38:36.199 --> 00:38:38.651
for conscious percept
experience to be generated,

00:38:38.651 --> 00:38:41.110
but you don't need
conscious percept experience

00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:45.321
to guide your actions as a
guided by this other system,

00:38:45.321 --> 00:38:48.290
that what later becomes identified

00:38:48.290 --> 00:38:51.103
as this vision for action pathway.

00:38:51.103 --> 00:38:54.570
So again, where these
results are said to show

00:38:54.570 --> 00:38:56.630
a clear dissociation
between motor responses

00:38:56.630 --> 00:38:58.780
and awareness like blindsight,

00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:00.850
and you know, this is from
a textbook this picture,

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:02.240
and that's what the textbook tells you.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:03.537
It's possible to disassociate behavior

00:39:03.537 --> 00:39:04.763
and conscious awareness.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:07.190
But I think there's a
much simpler explanation

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:09.685
of what's going on in these tasks.

00:39:09.685 --> 00:39:11.810
And to see this, just
reflect that in order

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:14.261
to accurately point or
reach out towards a target,

00:39:14.261 --> 00:39:16.820
you only have to represent
its current location.

00:39:16.820 --> 00:39:18.530
You don't care where it
was or where it will be.

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:21.810
You just need to know where
it is now in the moment,

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:25.050
but that does not suffice
for you to detect a change,

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:26.950
to detect a change you
need to do a whole bunch

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:28.660
of other things, especially if that change

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:31.930
has been hidden from you by
occurring during a saccade.

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:33.997
You need to encode the original
location of the target.

00:39:33.997 --> 00:39:35.768
You need to maintain that representation,

00:39:35.768 --> 00:39:39.447
you need to retrieve it when
the new occasion comes around

00:39:39.447 --> 00:39:41.723
and you need to compare
those two representations.

00:39:41.723 --> 00:39:44.524
So there's a whole sequence
of steps that need take place

00:39:44.524 --> 00:39:48.003
if you're going to accurately
judge there's been a change.

00:39:48.880 --> 00:39:50.876
Okay, and so to kind of
give you a sense of that,

00:39:50.876 --> 00:39:52.770
and if you've seen this
before, I apologize,

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:55.260
but I just want you to
indulge me by staring

00:39:55.260 --> 00:39:58.377
at the next slide for 30 seconds

00:39:58.377 --> 00:40:00.783
and see if you spot anything unusual.

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:44.960
Okay, so maybe I've spot
it by just saying that,

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:47.291
but this was the final slide you saw.

00:40:47.291 --> 00:40:49.708
And I dunno whether any of you realized

00:40:49.708 --> 00:40:53.382
who hadn't seen it before, but
this was the original slide.

00:40:53.382 --> 00:40:54.800
Okay, so massive change in color.

00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:55.799
If you're anything like me,

00:40:55.799 --> 00:40:58.670
you did not notice when
you first saw this, okay.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:00.363
But the last thing that you
want to conclude from this

00:41:00.363 --> 00:41:02.938
is that we don't see colors, okay?

00:41:02.938 --> 00:41:06.970
What this shows you
presumably is that you can

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:08.230
accurately represent the color

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:10.110
of something at any particular moment,

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:13.010
even though you fail across
time to do that whole procedure

00:41:13.010 --> 00:41:16.823
of maintaining, retrieving
and comparing accurately.

00:41:18.046 --> 00:41:20.000
Okay, so this is just a class example

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:22.080
of change blindness and
the point to be made

00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:23.650
about change blindness is
that the failures notice

00:41:23.650 --> 00:41:26.380
to the change doesn't in
itself mean you didn't see

00:41:26.380 --> 00:41:29.160
the particular feature
that changed at the moment

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:30.513
before or after the change.

00:41:31.370 --> 00:41:33.020
And I really think the same
thing is going on here.

00:41:33.020 --> 00:41:34.560
The failure to notice an extra jump

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:36.140
in the target doesn't mean that

00:41:36.140 --> 00:41:38.300
you don't consciously
perceive the target position

00:41:38.300 --> 00:41:40.282
effectively throughout the experiment.

00:41:40.282 --> 00:41:43.580
So there's not a clear demonstration here

00:41:43.580 --> 00:41:46.001
of a dissociation between
mechanisms mediating

00:41:46.001 --> 00:41:49.103
the perception of target
position and those mediating

00:41:49.103 --> 00:41:52.030
the visually guided reaching movements.

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:55.400
Both might be accurate representations

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:56.520
of the current target position,

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:58.520
what there's been a
failure of as a failure

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:01.674
to detect a change, but
that wasn't necessary to do

00:42:01.674 --> 00:42:03.790
the reaching task

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:08.190
and nor is it necessary to
see the accurate position

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:10.250
of the target consciously.

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:11.470
And the same I think is true here.

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:13.740
The course correction reflects
that you you've accurately

00:42:13.740 --> 00:42:16.840
updated the, your
representation of the target's

00:42:16.840 --> 00:42:18.532
current location, like where
the light currently is.

00:42:18.532 --> 00:42:21.590
But remember tah was
meant to be the signal

00:42:21.590 --> 00:42:23.600
of your conscious experience
of a visual change.

00:42:23.600 --> 00:42:26.830
So have you detected and are you,

00:42:26.830 --> 00:42:29.410
do you feel able to judge
that a change has occurred

00:42:29.410 --> 00:42:31.283
and you might then think
that that detection process

00:42:31.283 --> 00:42:33.714
requires some extra time go through some

00:42:33.714 --> 00:42:35.155
of those processing steps,

00:42:35.155 --> 00:42:37.390
the retrieval, the comparison and so on,

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:39.120
and that's what's explaining the delay

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:43.250
not the time needed for
conscious experience.

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:44.590
So again, I don't think there's evidence

00:42:44.590 --> 00:42:45.713
for dissociation here.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:51.952
Okay, let me now just finally talk about

00:42:51.952 --> 00:42:53.400
the second set of evidence.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:56.860
So and again this is
gonna be extremely brief

00:42:56.860 --> 00:43:00.080
and extremely narrow,
but I'm more than happy

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:02.348
to talk about other cases in the Q and A.

00:43:02.348 --> 00:43:06.039
So patient DF who has
this condition called

00:43:06.039 --> 00:43:10.760
visual form agnosia
suffered very badly actually

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:13.089
from carbon monoxide poisoning

00:43:13.089 --> 00:43:16.886
and had sort of extensive
lesions, but in particular,

00:43:16.886 --> 00:43:19.950
she has extensive lesions on both sides

00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:21.730
to her ventral stream.

00:43:21.730 --> 00:43:24.575
So remember the stream
that Milner and Goodale

00:43:24.575 --> 00:43:25.996
called vision for perception,

00:43:25.996 --> 00:43:27.410
that's associated with these
conscious representations

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:29.710
of her vision environment.

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:32.830
And she's not blind because
V1 was largely spared

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:36.110
and she can see color, she can see texture

00:43:36.110 --> 00:43:39.750
but she has real problems
with object recognition.

00:43:39.750 --> 00:43:42.740
She struggles to see size,
shape, orientation, and so forth.

00:43:42.740 --> 00:43:44.260
And just to give you some sense of that is

00:43:44.260 --> 00:43:48.084
an imaging study in which
she and here controls

00:43:48.084 --> 00:43:51.150
are shown these line drawings

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:55.645
and the activity elicited
in these ventral pathways

00:43:55.645 --> 00:43:57.740
when presented with these line drawings

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:01.362
is compared to the
scrambled versions here.

00:44:01.362 --> 00:44:03.060
And so in the control subjects,

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.570
you can see there's extensive
differential activity

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:07.501
in presence of this bed and this pepper

00:44:07.501 --> 00:44:10.110
compared to the scrambling, but in DF,

00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:15.110
there's absolutely no
differential activity to be seen.

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:17.010
And yet despite that, she appears

00:44:17.010 --> 00:44:19.500
to have really quite impressive abilities

00:44:19.500 --> 00:44:21.290
for visually guided action.

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:23.120
So let me just talk
about one particular case

00:44:23.120 --> 00:44:25.390
of that very famous experiment done

00:44:25.390 --> 00:44:28.090
by Milner and Goodale
back in the 90s, early 90s

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:33.090
where DF was just asked to
look at this orientable slot

00:44:33.357 --> 00:44:38.357
and do one of two things,
either to hold a card

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:41.360
and add a distance match
the orientation of the slot

00:44:41.360 --> 00:44:44.733
with the card, or reach out
and post it through the slot.

00:44:46.288 --> 00:44:49.900
Okay, and I'll show you a video
of her doing this in a sec,

00:44:49.900 --> 00:44:52.463
but here's the pattern of data.

00:44:52.463 --> 00:44:55.855
She's incredibly bad
at this matching task.

00:44:55.855 --> 00:44:59.049
These are kind of
normalized to the vertical,

00:44:59.049 --> 00:45:03.070
but you can see that her
performance there is almost random,

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:05.660
whereas in the vision motor posting task,

00:45:05.660 --> 00:45:08.980
she's not perfect or not
exactly like control subjects,

00:45:08.980 --> 00:45:11.022
but incredibly close.

00:45:11.022 --> 00:45:14.100
So this is the standard
story that's then told

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:15.430
about this pattern of data.

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:17.474
And here's, this is a
quotation from Ned Block.

00:45:17.474 --> 00:45:20.990
DF is almost as good as
posting as our normal controls,

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:22.700
but nearly random at matching.

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:23.947
What explains this, well,

00:45:23.947 --> 00:45:25.964
her conscious mind can't see orientation.

00:45:25.964 --> 00:45:28.062
That's why she can't do the matching task.

00:45:28.062 --> 00:45:31.430
Nonetheless, it must be the
orientation is perceived

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:32.847
otherwise how could she
do the posting task?

00:45:32.847 --> 00:45:34.400
Well, that must be perceived in

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:36.900
this unconscious action
guiding part of her brain.

00:45:38.655 --> 00:45:41.900
So here is her doing the matching task.

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:43.646
Let me just show you one example of that

00:45:43.646 --> 00:45:46.080
when you can already see,

00:45:46.080 --> 00:45:48.320
this is just wait for the experimenter

00:45:49.409 --> 00:45:51.123
to reorient the slot.

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:59.243
Okay, and now she's being asked to match.

00:46:02.540 --> 00:46:05.780
Okay so, and this goes on and every time

00:46:05.780 --> 00:46:07.220
there's really no relation

00:46:08.082 --> 00:46:09.450
you can see between the okay, but here's

00:46:09.450 --> 00:46:12.340
the more impressive thing, here is a case

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:14.830
of the slot being moved
and her being asked

00:46:14.830 --> 00:46:16.480
to reach out and post through it.

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:26.253
Okay, so really a remarkable dissociation.

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:30.700
Problem is again, there's
an alternative explanation

00:46:30.700 --> 00:46:31.650
of what's going on.

00:46:34.740 --> 00:46:36.640
And the thing is that needs recognizing

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:38.680
is that you don't need to be
able to perceive orientation

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:40.490
to do the posting task.

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:43.150
And that's 'cause there's
another way of doing it.

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:45.260
You can succeed in posting by using

00:46:45.260 --> 00:46:47.740
what's called an obstacle
avoidance strategy.

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:49.390
And what that involves is you seeking

00:46:49.390 --> 00:46:51.870
to minimize the distances
between the edges

00:46:51.870 --> 00:46:54.295
of the slot and the edges of the card,

00:46:54.295 --> 00:46:57.040
sorry, maximize the
distances, not minimize them.

00:46:57.040 --> 00:46:59.560
So if you can create maximum safety margin

00:46:59.560 --> 00:47:01.780
or, you know, room between

00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:04.150
the edges of the slot and
the edges of the card,

00:47:04.150 --> 00:47:06.190
you will post successfully.

00:47:06.190 --> 00:47:07.810
And in fact, you don't actually, you know,

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:10.361
orientation is kind of neither
necessary nor sufficient

00:47:10.361 --> 00:47:11.850
for doing the task.

00:47:11.850 --> 00:47:13.139
If you know the orientation,

00:47:13.139 --> 00:47:16.140
you still can't do the task
unless you know the midpoint,

00:47:16.140 --> 00:47:18.150
but if you don't know the orientation

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:22.693
and you do know like
that as held like this,

00:47:22.693 --> 00:47:25.660
you've managed to maximize
the distances between

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:26.900
the card and the slot edge,

00:47:26.900 --> 00:47:29.110
then you will be able to do the task.

00:47:29.110 --> 00:47:31.410
So this strategy is
strictly orientation free.

00:47:32.810 --> 00:47:33.930
Okay, but is there any evidence

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:35.580
this is what's actually going on?

00:47:36.690 --> 00:47:38.810
Well, the first piece of
evidence that was proposed

00:47:38.810 --> 00:47:40.920
for this account comes from a paper

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:42.745
by Hess, Franz and Schenk from 2011.

00:47:42.745 --> 00:47:45.540
And what they do is they modify the slot

00:47:45.540 --> 00:47:49.120
so that actually the orientation,

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:51.140
whether it's the maximum safety margin

00:47:51.140 --> 00:47:54.670
is not the vertical of the
slot, but it's diagonal.

00:47:54.670 --> 00:47:57.493
So it's a wide slot, but
they use very big cards.

00:47:58.730 --> 00:48:00.463
And what they find this is
just neurotypical subjects,

00:48:00.463 --> 00:48:02.520
is that the orientation era,

00:48:02.520 --> 00:48:07.080
namely the departure from that vertical

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:10.950
is pretty large in about
half of the subjects

00:48:10.950 --> 00:48:12.350
suggesting that about half of

00:48:12.350 --> 00:48:15.329
the subjects do in fact
post through this slot with

00:48:15.329 --> 00:48:20.280
a non, not orienting to
the slot's orientation,

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:22.280
but rather maximizing the safety margin.

00:48:23.623 --> 00:48:25.360
And interestingly, there's no relation

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:27.410
between their matching behavior
and whether they do that

00:48:27.410 --> 00:48:30.283
or whether they stick
to this strategy or not.

00:48:31.229 --> 00:48:33.029
So this is some suggestive evidence,

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:37.640
but in response to this,
Block in particular,

00:48:37.640 --> 00:48:39.370
but others too have
been rather unimpressed.

00:48:39.370 --> 00:48:41.280
They say, well, this is
just very weak evidence.

00:48:41.280 --> 00:48:44.440
This is a very weak criticism
of the original study

00:48:44.440 --> 00:48:45.273
for three reasons.

00:48:45.273 --> 00:48:48.407
One, look that a task that's
been deliberately designed to

00:48:48.407 --> 00:48:51.093
elicit obstacle avoidance
does so in normal subjects,

00:48:51.093 --> 00:48:53.443
doesn't show anything
about what DF is doing.

00:48:54.380 --> 00:48:57.510
Second, as you saw in
the video I showed you,

00:48:57.510 --> 00:48:59.476
DF moves our hand forward unhesitatingly

00:48:59.476 --> 00:49:01.930
and almost always
inserts the card smoothly

00:49:01.930 --> 00:49:03.521
into the slot, Block says,

00:49:03.521 --> 00:49:05.840
and she also begins to
rotate the card towards

00:49:05.840 --> 00:49:09.003
the correct orientation when
in advance of arriving at it.

00:49:10.550 --> 00:49:12.770
And finally he says that he
thinks orientation perception

00:49:12.770 --> 00:49:14.220
would be required to avoid hitting

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:16.040
the edge of the slot with the card.

00:49:16.040 --> 00:49:18.310
Now I just think that's not
correct, although I don't,

00:49:18.310 --> 00:49:19.370
he doesn't say any more than this.

00:49:19.370 --> 00:49:21.627
So I don't know precisely the thinking,

00:49:21.627 --> 00:49:24.290
but I do not think it's true
that you need orientation

00:49:24.290 --> 00:49:25.653
to succeed in this task.

00:49:26.596 --> 00:49:29.247
With respect to this criticism,

00:49:29.247 --> 00:49:32.665
I think this would be
correct as a criticism

00:49:32.665 --> 00:49:36.480
if there was somehow an
obvious difference between

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:38.130
the subjects who did,

00:49:38.130 --> 00:49:40.435
who used the strategy and the Hess paper,

00:49:40.435 --> 00:49:43.310
but actually they also, if
you look at the details,

00:49:43.310 --> 00:49:47.590
all had adjusted the card
to that as it were diagonal

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:50.561
orientation by midway
through their trajectories.

00:49:50.561 --> 00:49:53.880
And they all posted
smoothly and effortlessly

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:55.160
in a similar way.

00:49:55.160 --> 00:49:57.851
So it really the key point here is that

00:49:57.851 --> 00:50:00.080
this difference in
strategy doesn't predict

00:50:00.080 --> 00:50:02.970
that you wouldn't be able to do a smooth

00:50:02.970 --> 00:50:04.990
posting movement or that
you wouldn't be able to get

00:50:04.990 --> 00:50:06.790
the orientation in advance.

00:50:06.790 --> 00:50:09.690
It's just a difference in
the information being used.

00:50:09.690 --> 00:50:11.521
But the first point is I
think absolutely right,

00:50:11.521 --> 00:50:14.523
that we can't generalize too
much from this early study,

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:16.800
but I now think there's stronger evidence.

00:50:16.800 --> 00:50:18.553
And this comes from the same group

00:50:18.553 --> 00:50:21.281
from a much more recent paper.

00:50:21.281 --> 00:50:25.623
And it exploits an effect
known as the oblique effect.

00:50:27.270 --> 00:50:30.840
So all of us are reliably more accurate

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:33.312
in judging horizontal
and vertical orientations

00:50:33.312 --> 00:50:36.590
than oblique orientations.

00:50:36.590 --> 00:50:38.340
So, you know, cardinal
orientations are just,

00:50:38.340 --> 00:50:39.900
if you look around the room

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:41.580
you'll see they're all over the place.

00:50:41.580 --> 00:50:42.900
We're very, very used to them.

00:50:42.900 --> 00:50:44.340
And our visual systems
are very used to them.

00:50:44.340 --> 00:50:45.530
So we're better at discriminating

00:50:45.530 --> 00:50:48.972
horizontals and vertical as
than oblique orientations.

00:50:48.972 --> 00:50:53.220
So if posting were to
be based on orientation,

00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:55.573
you'd expect it to show an oblique effect.

00:50:56.577 --> 00:50:59.440
If it's based on an
obstacle avoidance strategy,

00:50:59.440 --> 00:51:02.030
which is orientation-free, you wouldn't.

00:51:02.030 --> 00:51:03.740
So just as proof of concept,

00:51:03.740 --> 00:51:06.490
here's evidence from a matching paradigm

00:51:06.490 --> 00:51:08.430
which does show an oblique effect.

00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:10.660
So in both these two conditions here,

00:51:10.660 --> 00:51:12.850
the open loop means the
subjects can't see the target

00:51:12.850 --> 00:51:14.110
as soon as they've begun the movement.

00:51:14.110 --> 00:51:15.820
So that's why the errors are larger.

00:51:15.820 --> 00:51:20.040
You're seeing errors in the
matching of an orientation

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:21.340
and you can see they're much lower with

00:51:21.340 --> 00:51:24.393
the horizontal and verticals
here than with the obliques.

00:51:25.870 --> 00:51:29.053
Without posting no such effect.

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:33.470
Okay, there is a pattern
in this data though,

00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:34.890
and which some of you know

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:37.990
that will have spotted looks like errors

00:51:37.990 --> 00:51:39.970
are increasing in this direction.

00:51:39.970 --> 00:51:41.610
Well that's because they
were started from a,

00:51:41.610 --> 00:51:44.979
at the same kind of post
horizontal rotation position.

00:51:44.979 --> 00:51:49.480
So we can fix that as
the experimentalists did

00:51:49.480 --> 00:51:51.090
by using variable starting positions.

00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:52.760
And we can also do one other
thing that's important.

00:51:52.760 --> 00:51:55.970
We can introduce a delay
between the presentation

00:51:55.970 --> 00:51:58.520
of the target and the action being made.

00:51:58.520 --> 00:52:01.089
And that's important because
it's natural to worry

00:52:01.089 --> 00:52:04.692
or think that maybe the two
visual systems people will say,

00:52:04.692 --> 00:52:06.340
but maybe the dorsal stream,

00:52:06.340 --> 00:52:08.130
maybe our action guiding
stream doesn't have

00:52:08.130 --> 00:52:09.990
an oblique effect associated with it.

00:52:09.990 --> 00:52:12.040
Maybe that's just a ventral stream thing,

00:52:12.968 --> 00:52:15.700
But those people, Goodale
and Milner do think

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:17.812
that once you introduce
a delay are actions

00:52:17.812 --> 00:52:20.716
fall back on ventral
stream representations.

00:52:20.716 --> 00:52:23.410
So action guiding
representations are supposed

00:52:23.410 --> 00:52:27.010
to be kind of on the fly, in
the moment representations

00:52:27.010 --> 00:52:28.710
which are used in online guidance,

00:52:29.606 --> 00:52:30.720
but when we are falling back on memory

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:32.250
we use the ventral stream.

00:52:32.250 --> 00:52:37.250
So this delay ought to definitely
get us an oblique effect

00:52:37.770 --> 00:52:39.970
if there is one to be found, of course,

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:42.530
here's the matching data
again, showing such an effect,

00:52:42.530 --> 00:52:45.453
but here's the posting data
again showing no such effect.

00:52:46.710 --> 00:52:48.562
So this is all data from
neurotypical subjects,

00:52:48.562 --> 00:52:51.409
but what is really suggesting
is that neurotypical subjects

00:52:51.409 --> 00:52:55.270
don't use orientation
information primarily

00:52:55.270 --> 00:52:58.940
in guiding the posting
we're using obstacle

00:52:58.940 --> 00:53:00.093
avoidance strategies.

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:04.960
Okay so in general, subjects use

00:53:04.960 --> 00:53:07.720
orientation-free obstacle
avoidance strategies, or at least,

00:53:07.720 --> 00:53:10.070
I mean really is evidence
to their orientation-free,

00:53:10.070 --> 00:53:11.640
not specifically that
they're obstacle avoidance,

00:53:11.640 --> 00:53:13.461
but in their posting.

00:53:13.461 --> 00:53:15.920
And so our default
hypothesis now should be

00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:17.123
that DF is doing that.

00:53:19.748 --> 00:53:20.810
And if that's right then her performance

00:53:20.810 --> 00:53:22.560
on the posting class
clearly doesn't establish

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:24.360
that she's perceived the
orientation of the slot

00:53:24.360 --> 00:53:26.420
in an unconscious action
guiding part of her brain.

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:27.430
The simplest thing to say is that

00:53:27.430 --> 00:53:28.983
she just can't see orientation.

00:53:31.010 --> 00:53:35.280
Okay, I'm over the time that
I said I would speak for

00:53:35.280 --> 00:53:36.383
so I'm gonna wrap up now,

00:53:36.383 --> 00:53:38.823
but there's a huge number
of other bits of evidence

00:53:38.823 --> 00:53:40.610
that I could have mentioned

00:53:40.610 --> 00:53:43.260
but this is a real take home
I want to leave you with

00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:48.950
What I've argued is that
across a very diverse range

00:53:48.950 --> 00:53:52.870
of cases and for a whole
set of different reasons,

00:53:52.870 --> 00:53:55.440
these apparently striking
dissociations of performance

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:58.204
and awareness that you have
seen in blindsight and neglect

00:53:58.204 --> 00:54:03.204
may in fact be spacious or
artifactual in different ways.

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:05.320
So in the case of blindsight and neglect

00:54:05.320 --> 00:54:06.590
it was the problem with the criterion,

00:54:06.590 --> 00:54:09.818
which I pointed to in the
case of subliminal priming,

00:54:09.818 --> 00:54:11.950
it's not so much the effect is suspicious

00:54:11.950 --> 00:54:13.100
or artifact there is that

00:54:13.100 --> 00:54:16.420
the there's a gap between
the data and what it shows

00:54:16.420 --> 00:54:19.520
and the anything that the
golden braid is talking about,

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:22.135
namely perception by us and our actions.

00:54:22.135 --> 00:54:25.614
In the case of those visual
motor action studies,

00:54:25.614 --> 00:54:27.900
the point was that there's a conflation

00:54:27.900 --> 00:54:31.710
or a misunderstanding
of what is being shown

00:54:32.605 --> 00:54:33.438
by failure to detect change.

00:54:33.438 --> 00:54:36.400
It doesn't show that we're
not tracking a feature

00:54:36.400 --> 00:54:37.830
And in the case of visual form agnosia,

00:54:37.830 --> 00:54:39.210
and this would apply to many other studies

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:41.910
of visual form agnosia, there
are just different strategies

00:54:41.910 --> 00:54:44.050
that can be used to do different tasks.

00:54:44.050 --> 00:54:46.640
And so when you compare two
tasks, you need to be very,

00:54:46.640 --> 00:54:50.823
very sure that the same strategy
is being applied in both.

00:54:52.530 --> 00:54:54.480
Okay, this then returns
us to this naive vision

00:54:54.480 --> 00:54:57.352
I began with, which Clark paints

00:54:57.352 --> 00:54:59.730
on which perception is always conscious

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:01.550
and always available for action guidance

00:55:01.550 --> 00:55:06.210
and all action guidance occurs
with conscious perception.

00:55:06.210 --> 00:55:08.820
And I'm not really
standing here today saying,

00:55:08.820 --> 00:55:10.340
this is the truth.

00:55:10.340 --> 00:55:12.290
What I am trying to stand here and say is,

00:55:12.290 --> 00:55:13.520
this is our starting point

00:55:13.520 --> 00:55:16.230
and we shouldn't abandon it
without extremely good evidence.

00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:18.300
And the evidence that I've surveyed,

00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:20.500
I don't think is decisive evidence.

00:55:20.500 --> 00:55:23.346
So not everything that's
naive or intuitive

00:55:23.346 --> 00:55:26.750
should be given up upon.

00:55:26.750 --> 00:55:28.720
Okay, thank you very much for listening.

00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:31.970
(audience applauding)

00:55:36.130 --> 00:55:38.570
- [Lady] So thank you, Ian.

00:55:38.570 --> 00:55:40.789
That was fantastic and we'll now open

00:55:40.789 --> 00:55:44.543
the floor for a few questions.

00:55:50.200 --> 00:55:51.435
- [Man] Thank you very much, Ian,

00:55:51.435 --> 00:55:53.490
this extremely interesting paper.

00:55:53.490 --> 00:55:55.180
In your debate with Ned Block,

00:55:55.180 --> 00:55:57.240
I find myself wondering what you regard

00:55:57.240 --> 00:55:58.670
as the minimal condition

00:55:58.670 --> 00:55:59.710
would it been true that something

00:55:59.710 --> 00:56:02.237
is conscious or aware of anything?

00:56:02.237 --> 00:56:04.840
It seems though you and
Block had a clear idea

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:06.850
of what it was to be
consciously aware of something.

00:56:06.850 --> 00:56:08.320
And you're arguing about that,

00:56:08.320 --> 00:56:10.960
but looking at the data
one might wonder whether

00:56:10.960 --> 00:56:12.751
first of all, awareness
isn't a matter of degree.

00:56:12.751 --> 00:56:14.911
Secondly, whether it is vague.

00:56:14.911 --> 00:56:19.480
And thirdly, whether there
is a determinate cutoff point

00:56:19.480 --> 00:56:21.120
at the bottom level.

00:56:21.120 --> 00:56:22.310
So I was wondering whether you could say

00:56:22.310 --> 00:56:23.750
what you regarded the minimal condition

00:56:23.750 --> 00:56:25.863
for the presence of consciousness to be.

00:56:27.380 --> 00:56:28.620
- Yeah, thanks that, I mean,

00:56:28.620 --> 00:56:31.690
that's a huge question with
many questions built into it.

00:56:31.690 --> 00:56:34.470
So maybe one answer is just,

00:56:34.470 --> 00:56:36.470
I can't give you an
answer to that question.

00:56:36.470 --> 00:56:39.400
So I think there is a slight
difference between us and a,

00:56:39.400 --> 00:56:40.580
but also a bit of agreement.

00:56:40.580 --> 00:56:43.420
So I think we're both agreed the interest

00:56:43.420 --> 00:56:46.446
is in phenomenal
consciousness, both Ned and I,

00:56:46.446 --> 00:56:49.641
but I am inclined to impose
a kind of access condition

00:56:49.641 --> 00:56:52.927
on that, which was an action
condition I mentioned.

00:56:52.927 --> 00:56:55.248
So I guess I think one condition

00:56:55.248 --> 00:56:58.520
that is a plausible condition for

00:56:58.520 --> 00:57:01.100
a representation being
conscious is that it's,

00:57:01.100 --> 00:57:03.640
you know, usable by the
subject in some way,

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:07.350
and I don't think that
the Ned would necessarily,

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:09.170
well, I'm pretty sure he
wouldn't agree with that.

00:57:09.170 --> 00:57:11.470
He might be happy with the idea that there

00:57:12.656 --> 00:57:14.219
are conscious representations that just

00:57:14.219 --> 00:57:15.052
are completely inaccessible.

00:57:17.280 --> 00:57:19.883
Then there's the question of degrees.

00:57:22.860 --> 00:57:24.990
I mean, I do think so a way
in which we can make sense

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:28.640
of consciousness or at
least of a certain kind

00:57:28.640 --> 00:57:30.924
of episode as being
more or less conscious,

00:57:30.924 --> 00:57:34.400
but I also think there's that,

00:57:34.400 --> 00:57:37.070
that's not inconsistent with
it being either true or false

00:57:37.070 --> 00:57:40.074
that it is a conscious episode
or not, see what I mean?

00:57:40.074 --> 00:57:41.270
So when we talk about degraded vision

00:57:41.270 --> 00:57:42.830
or a very, very weak signal,

00:57:42.830 --> 00:57:45.050
I think that gives us
some operational grip

00:57:45.050 --> 00:57:50.050
on it being a conscious to
a low degree as it were,

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:53.270
but I still think it does a matter of fact

00:57:53.270 --> 00:57:55.899
about whether it's conscious or not.

00:57:55.899 --> 00:57:58.460
And then, you know,
might it be a determinant

00:57:58.460 --> 00:58:00.506
or might it be vague?

00:58:00.506 --> 00:58:03.950
I don't want to rule that out at all.

00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:06.720
Nothing I've said is committed to that.

00:58:06.720 --> 00:58:08.665
And I don't really know if that's,

00:58:08.665 --> 00:58:11.410
there's anything in our
naive or intuitive view

00:58:11.410 --> 00:58:16.410
which says there's always a
determinant fact of the matter,

00:58:16.763 --> 00:58:21.763
but I mean, feel free to press me more

00:58:22.050 --> 00:58:23.450
on things I've just said or.

00:58:27.493 --> 00:58:30.910
- [Lady] There is one question, Mike.

00:58:32.417 --> 00:58:35.408
- [Mike] I wanted to, I find
this all very convincing,

00:58:35.408 --> 00:58:38.556
but I'm not familiar with
debates but still convincing,

00:58:38.556 --> 00:58:41.273
but what do you think motivates people

00:58:41.273 --> 00:58:45.820
like Block to insist that
performance and awareness

00:58:45.820 --> 00:58:47.328
can be disassociated?

00:58:47.328 --> 00:58:51.413
What's in it for them as it
were in taking this line?

00:58:51.413 --> 00:58:53.389
Is there a philosophical motivation

00:58:53.389 --> 00:58:56.353
or is there something that
they're not concerned with

00:58:56.353 --> 00:58:57.610
that you are concerned with,

00:58:57.610 --> 00:58:59.593
that can explain this difference?

00:59:01.150 --> 00:59:02.873
- That's a really interesting question.

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:06.970
I don't, I mean, I feel
like I shouldn't try

00:59:06.970 --> 00:59:11.770
and parenter Ned, so, but
I kind of think there's a,

00:59:11.770 --> 00:59:13.510
it's particularly interesting in his case

00:59:13.510 --> 00:59:15.540
because he has this distinction

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:17.640
between phenomenal and
access consciousness.

00:59:17.640 --> 00:59:19.580
And once you've got that
distinction, you might think,

00:59:19.580 --> 00:59:21.340
well, why not think that
many of these cases,

00:59:21.340 --> 00:59:24.130
the cases are phenomenal
without some form of access?

00:59:24.130 --> 00:59:24.980
So that was in ways,

00:59:24.980 --> 00:59:26.617
the question that first got
me interested in this area,

00:59:26.617 --> 00:59:31.090
and it's kind of a question for Ned.

00:59:31.090 --> 00:59:32.886
So he has nonetheless though,

00:59:32.886 --> 00:59:36.770
decided that as he sees the evidence,

00:59:36.770 --> 00:59:39.320
it points to a kind of
tripartite distinction

00:59:39.320 --> 00:59:42.120
between this unconscious
truly unconscious,

00:59:42.120 --> 00:59:45.020
phenomenally conscious
and access consciousness.

00:59:45.020 --> 00:59:48.120
I mean, I do think he's seriously guided

00:59:48.120 --> 00:59:49.620
by the empirical evidence in this case.

00:59:49.620 --> 00:59:52.640
And so I think that's what he would reply

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:54.220
and very reasonably, so many,

00:59:54.220 --> 00:59:56.890
many people actually
with very different views

00:59:56.890 --> 01:00:00.943
of consciousness to his,
see these cases as being,

01:00:02.600 --> 01:00:05.453
absolutely clear demonstrations of things.

01:00:08.360 --> 01:00:13.360
So, I mean, in ways I think there's a kind

01:00:13.660 --> 01:00:17.410
of a really legitimate
deference to science

01:00:17.410 --> 01:00:19.182
that's going on in a lot of philosophy,

01:00:19.182 --> 01:00:22.650
but I think there's also
an efficient attention

01:00:22.650 --> 01:00:23.790
to the assumptions that are going

01:00:23.790 --> 01:00:26.620
into the interpretation of these
studies, which is what I'm,

01:00:26.620 --> 01:00:30.520
so maybe there shouldn't have
been quite so much in my view.

01:00:37.900 --> 01:00:40.110
- [Lady] Hi, thank you
so much for the talk.

01:00:40.110 --> 01:00:42.270
I have to confess, I'm
very new to all of this.

01:00:42.270 --> 01:00:45.980
So I wanted to ask a very basic question.

01:00:45.980 --> 01:00:48.100
So it seemed that the golden
braid is tying together

01:00:48.100 --> 01:00:51.860
three things, perception,
conscious perception,

01:00:51.860 --> 01:00:53.600
and action guidingness.

01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:56.470
And it seemed like the debates
over the evidence was about

01:00:56.470 --> 01:00:59.310
whether it's conscious
perception that's action guiding,

01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:01.040
or whether we have unconscious perception

01:01:01.040 --> 01:01:02.950
that's action guiding.

01:01:02.950 --> 01:01:04.440
And I wondered if you could say something

01:01:04.440 --> 01:01:07.500
about the link between
perception and acting guidingness

01:01:09.062 --> 01:01:12.900
in general, such that, I'm not quite sure

01:01:12.900 --> 01:01:13.733
how to articulate it.

01:01:13.733 --> 01:01:15.120
Can the two be pulled apart,

01:01:15.120 --> 01:01:16.803
or is there something
about the way in which

01:01:16.803 --> 01:01:19.263
we investigate psychologically where

01:01:19.263 --> 01:01:21.560
it can't be pulled apart
because we're looking

01:01:21.560 --> 01:01:23.900
at people's behaviors and as we ruled out

01:01:23.900 --> 01:01:28.790
the subjective measures,
what happens there?

01:01:28.790 --> 01:01:30.975
- Yeah, that's a really good question.

01:01:30.975 --> 01:01:31.808
I mean, I think there's something

01:01:31.808 --> 01:01:33.869
slightly terminological here.

01:01:33.869 --> 01:01:38.869
So when Milner and Goodale
say that this visual stream

01:01:40.000 --> 01:01:41.570
that guides action is vision for action,

01:01:41.570 --> 01:01:43.618
they contrast it with
vision for perception.

01:01:43.618 --> 01:01:45.255
So as the they're
talking about perception,

01:01:45.255 --> 01:01:47.460
they're talking about
something that's conscious

01:01:47.460 --> 01:01:48.680
or at least potentially conscious.

01:01:48.680 --> 01:01:52.730
So then the question
is all action guidance.

01:01:52.730 --> 01:01:55.790
Perceptual is a real one
because we're getting

01:01:55.790 --> 01:01:59.831
this contrast between
information that's coming in,

01:01:59.831 --> 01:02:01.852
as it were through the census,

01:02:01.852 --> 01:02:05.880
that's genuinely
perceptual and information,

01:02:05.880 --> 01:02:08.848
which isn't because it's going
with this dorsal pathway.

01:02:08.848 --> 01:02:13.306
And my first move was to say,
as part of the golden braid,

01:02:13.306 --> 01:02:16.074
all perception is conscious.

01:02:16.074 --> 01:02:18.853
And then you can't, the
contrast that they have

01:02:18.853 --> 01:02:20.986
can't exist at that point.

01:02:20.986 --> 01:02:23.440
So your question then becomes
well, is there anything else,

01:02:23.440 --> 01:02:25.088
is there any other source of information

01:02:25.088 --> 01:02:29.743
that could be action guiding
that wasn't perceptual?

01:02:33.510 --> 01:02:36.490
No, I actually think that
it's not totally obvious

01:02:36.490 --> 01:02:38.710
there isn't, but it would be some kind of,

01:02:38.710 --> 01:02:40.990
as it were extra sensory information

01:02:40.990 --> 01:02:43.810
that in an with guiding our
actions in an online way,

01:02:43.810 --> 01:02:46.040
and I don't know what
exactly that would be.

01:02:46.040 --> 01:02:50.983
So, I'm not actually certain
it's in the golden braid

01:02:54.970 --> 01:02:56.440
that, that's all ruled out,

01:02:56.440 --> 01:02:58.950
but I don't think it's part
of our naive conception

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:01.484
that there are other ways
of information coming in

01:03:01.484 --> 01:03:05.070
that aren't via the
sensors that are available

01:03:05.070 --> 01:03:05.903
for action guidance.

01:03:05.903 --> 01:03:07.630
Of course, there's
information that's stored

01:03:07.630 --> 01:03:10.390
and used independently
of current perception

01:03:10.390 --> 01:03:12.654
that guides action, we can act as it were

01:03:12.654 --> 01:03:14.723
on the basis of that.

01:03:15.750 --> 01:03:17.400
Did you have anything in mind or.

01:03:18.780 --> 01:03:20.040
- [Lady] No, I didn't
have anything in mind,

01:03:20.040 --> 01:03:21.480
but I was wondering,

01:03:21.480 --> 01:03:25.844
so the direction you're
going is whether perception,

01:03:25.844 --> 01:03:29.420
like what are the sources
for action guidingness?

01:03:29.420 --> 01:03:31.640
And I was wondering about
the other direction,

01:03:31.640 --> 01:03:33.370
such that could you just have perception

01:03:33.370 --> 01:03:35.710
that isn't action guiding in some way,

01:03:35.710 --> 01:03:37.290
or maybe I just dunno what it means

01:03:37.290 --> 01:03:40.376
for something to be action
guiding in the relevant sense.

01:03:40.376 --> 01:03:41.548
- Yeah, you you're right.

01:03:41.548 --> 01:03:42.609
So that the kind of cases that someone

01:03:42.609 --> 01:03:43.900
like Block might point
to where alleged cases,

01:03:43.900 --> 01:03:45.341
where you have perception,

01:03:45.341 --> 01:03:48.779
conscious perception,
but it's inaccessible

01:03:48.779 --> 01:03:53.779
might count as cases where
there's those representations,

01:03:55.670 --> 01:03:57.533
although they contribute to
what it's like to be you,

01:03:57.533 --> 01:04:02.533
they're conscious are not
available for guiding actions.

01:04:03.408 --> 01:04:05.998
I happen to think there
aren't any obvious such cases,

01:04:05.998 --> 01:04:10.330
but that's not really
something I spoke in,

01:04:10.330 --> 01:04:11.980
at all significantly about today.

01:04:13.590 --> 01:04:15.650
- [Lady] Let's all right,

01:04:15.650 --> 01:04:17.640
all right Paul, one last question.

01:04:17.640 --> 01:04:18.473
(audience laughing)

01:04:18.473 --> 01:04:20.057
- [Paul] Thanks.

01:04:20.057 --> 01:04:21.933
I'll try to make it quick.

01:04:21.933 --> 01:04:25.110
It's a methodological question.

01:04:25.110 --> 01:04:28.410
Where are the qualitative data
sources on these conditions?

01:04:28.410 --> 01:04:30.780
I thought it might be much
easier to adjudicate between

01:04:30.780 --> 01:04:32.913
the golden braid view and Block's view

01:04:32.913 --> 01:04:35.700
by just asking people who have blindsight,

01:04:35.700 --> 01:04:37.550
what is your experience like rather

01:04:37.550 --> 01:04:39.710
than putting them through
these complicated tests?

01:04:39.710 --> 01:04:41.130
So where's the qualitative data here?

01:04:41.130 --> 01:04:43.900
Is there some barrier to
interpreting qualitative data

01:04:43.900 --> 01:04:48.350
that is not easy to see, or, yeah?

01:04:48.350 --> 01:04:49.873
- Yeah, so that's a good question.

01:04:49.873 --> 01:04:54.873
So I have looked very
hard to kind of assemble,

01:04:55.726 --> 01:04:58.430
you know, subjective
reports of what it's like

01:04:58.430 --> 01:04:59.740
to have blindsight in the literature.

01:04:59.740 --> 01:05:00.920
And there are a bunch actually.

01:05:00.920 --> 01:05:03.790
So the claim that GY makes on interview

01:05:03.790 --> 01:05:05.190
that he's lost all vision to his right,

01:05:05.190 --> 01:05:08.593
is undermined by his own reports
when he says things like,

01:05:10.140 --> 01:05:12.040
you know, sometimes I can see orientation,

01:05:12.040 --> 01:05:15.050
but then I lose all form or, you know,

01:05:15.050 --> 01:05:18.183
as if there's some kind of failures of,

01:05:18.183 --> 01:05:20.960
might describe it as like a
failure of binding of features

01:05:20.960 --> 01:05:22.920
that you can see one type
of feature at a time.

01:05:22.920 --> 01:05:24.750
He sometimes talks about how his vision

01:05:24.750 --> 01:05:29.750
is a bit like what a normally sighted,

01:05:30.310 --> 01:05:34.506
well, he uses the word
man, would experience

01:05:34.506 --> 01:05:37.505
if they were looking out at a bright light

01:05:37.505 --> 01:05:40.480
with their eyes closed and wave their hand

01:05:40.480 --> 01:05:43.019
between the light and their
hand or something like that.

01:05:43.019 --> 01:05:46.500
And he uses these lovely
phrases, like black on black,

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:47.993
like a mouse under a blanket.

01:05:47.993 --> 01:05:50.070
So he has these evocative descriptions

01:05:50.070 --> 01:05:50.903
of his own experience.

01:05:50.903 --> 01:05:52.520
So there is qualitative work and it's also

01:05:52.520 --> 01:05:53.736
this amazing paper though, that

01:05:53.736 --> 01:05:58.736
I think really does it expose
what might be problematic

01:05:59.720 --> 01:06:00.920
about some of this data.

01:06:01.830 --> 01:06:03.760
So it's actually, it's a Hackman Lao paper

01:06:03.760 --> 01:06:06.600
and it's a super interesting approach,

01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:08.600
but I think at least the
philosophers in the room

01:06:08.600 --> 01:06:10.060
will smile at it.

01:06:10.060 --> 01:06:13.230
So what they decided they
needed to do with patient GY

01:06:13.230 --> 01:06:15.530
who's really the most
extensively study paper,

01:06:15.530 --> 01:06:17.451
was get GY to understand what quality are.

01:06:17.451 --> 01:06:20.451
(audience laughing)

01:06:20.451 --> 01:06:23.080
So they got these passages from, you know,

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:24.541
the Stanford Encyclopedia Philosophy

01:06:24.541 --> 01:06:25.374
(audience laughing)

01:06:25.374 --> 01:06:29.101
from like Jackson and Thai and
whoever and said, read this.

01:06:29.101 --> 01:06:30.425
And he's a very dedicated subject

01:06:30.425 --> 01:06:32.950
and reads his own literature.

01:06:32.950 --> 01:06:35.312
So he read the philosophical literature

01:06:35.312 --> 01:06:37.550
and do you have these?

01:06:37.550 --> 01:06:39.850
And if you look at what he was given,

01:06:39.850 --> 01:06:42.210
he was actually given these
kinds of the bright blue

01:06:42.210 --> 01:06:44.190
of the Pacific Ocean that, you know,

01:06:44.190 --> 01:06:46.040
the fresh smell of coffee in the morning,

01:06:46.040 --> 01:06:47.470
kind of descriptions of quality.

01:06:47.470 --> 01:06:49.840
And so what he said was, no, I don't.

01:06:49.840 --> 01:06:52.693
Except on the very like high
contrast trials basically.

01:06:56.103 --> 01:06:58.955
So I just think this
problem that criterion

01:06:58.955 --> 01:07:03.570
is present in any kind
of subjective report.

01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:05.053
It's just the psychophysical work,

01:07:05.053 --> 01:07:09.443
uses it in a much narrow and
more controlled environment.

01:07:11.070 --> 01:07:13.538
- [Lady] So let's thank (indistinct).

01:07:13.538 --> 01:07:16.788
(audience applauding)

